WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.387
today we're going to be speaking with&nbsp;
Nicole Purlie, Roth, who's an award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.387 --> 00:00:08.880
winning cybersecurity journalist for The New York&nbsp;
Times and also author of This Is How They Tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.240
Me The World Ends the Cyber Weapons Arms Race.
It's so great to have you on. I appreciate it.&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.360
Thanks so much for having me, David.
So let's just start kind of at the top level.&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.320
I think a lot of people who are&nbsp;
aware of a cyber threat and about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.440
priority that this has become for so many&nbsp;
governments may not really understand what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.800
a cyber attack might actually mean sort of for the&nbsp;
average person. You might have a vague idea about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.720
could you shut down a water system, for example,&nbsp;
or paralyze an electricity grid or something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.480
that? What do we mean by cyber attack? What&nbsp;
are the types of things that could be done?&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.880
So for a long time, people's real experience&nbsp;
with cyber attacks was really just the theft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.120
of their personal data or maybe their credit&nbsp;
card information, and there was no real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.600
repercussions there. You know, you might have to&nbsp;
deal with identity theft if it got really gnarly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.840
But for the most part, your bank would probably&nbsp;
pay you back if any money was stolen from your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.760
account or credit card information, that&nbsp;
kind of thing. The problem is that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.920
days we have rolled so much software into our&nbsp;
critical infrastructure, like the power grid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.880
like our nuclear plants, like our water treatment&nbsp;
facilities and wastewater treatment plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:30.640
that all of that those access points can be&nbsp;
the target for a cyber attack. And in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.080
we are seeing every year more nation&nbsp;
state hackers, but also low level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.520
cyber criminals target these systems.&nbsp;
And so the attack that we really fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.440
would be an attack that took out power and&nbsp;
potentially contaminated the water. And actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:55.200
it would look a lot like what we just witnessed in&nbsp;
Texas a few weeks ago when the power went out, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:03.200
water supply was contaminated. And in the event of&nbsp;
a cyber attack, which you could use cyber means to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.920
take out the power and contaminate the water. And&nbsp;
we've seen Russia do that in Ukraine and we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:14.240
saw attempt to contaminate the water supply in a&nbsp;
small town in Florida the other week. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.960
it was caught. But the problem is with the&nbsp;
cyber attack, there's also the possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:26.160
that hackers could keep those systems down. And&nbsp;
when we've run studies here in the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:32.160
to see how resilient our critical infrastructure&nbsp;
is to cyber attack, some of those simulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.880
have showed that it could be years before people&nbsp;
were able to bring those systems back online. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.160
I think we're a little bit better than we were&nbsp;
when those simulations were done. But there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:49.760
a real threat of a cyber attack on on the grid.
So when it comes to the kind of identity theft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.640
credit card theft stuff that many of us have&nbsp;
dealt with, all of a sudden I had a period where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.800
like Uber and Dauda, charges were showing up on my&nbsp;
credit card and the annoyance type stuff, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.560
the motivation of the individual is basically to&nbsp;
get free stuff, to get a free, free takeout or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.440
free Uber ride or whatever the case may be. When&nbsp;
we start talking about things like shutting down a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.960
power grid, contaminating water, et cetera, it's&nbsp;
correct me if I'm wrong. It seems we're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.880
about a fork that is more malicious for the sake&nbsp;
of being malicious, of making a political point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:29.120
of of making an attack that may be more against&nbsp;
an institution or a country or an organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.520
Are those these two forks in terms of&nbsp;
motivations or is it more complicated than that?&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.760
Well, I think you're right. I mean, a lot of the&nbsp;
attacks we have seen on critical infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:48.400
has been messaging. When Russia shut off the&nbsp;
power in Ukraine, first in 2015 and then in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.600
they only did it for a few hours. And I&nbsp;
really believe that that was to send a message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.400
to sort of see doubt. And Ukrainians mind that&nbsp;
their new government had things under control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.720
It was really political messaging and sort of&nbsp;
hanging this threat over the population that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.280
Russia could turn off the power at the flip&nbsp;
of a switch. Other attacks have had different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:17.200
motivations. We caught Iran rifling around&nbsp;
the controls at a dam and the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:22.400
fortunately, it was the wrong dam. We have to&nbsp;
bowmen dams. One of them holds back us dunamis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:28.960
worth of water and organ, and the other holds back&nbsp;
a babbling brook in New York. And they got into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.680
the one that holds back a babbling brook and it&nbsp;
was under repair. So it wasn't an actual threat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.520
But, you know, who knows what the message was&nbsp;
there? I think it was probably just some sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:46.640
of experimentation to see if they could get into&nbsp;
the systems in case they needed to one day unleash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:52.240
the locks at that dam. And that's more of what&nbsp;
we're starting to see. We're starting to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.080
nation state hackers get into these&nbsp;
critical infrastructure systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.520
not necessarily because they&nbsp;
want to do something immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:08.720
but because we're all planning for some kind of&nbsp;
geopolitical trigger where we might need to be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:14.400
a position to turn off the lights or unleash the&nbsp;
locks at a dam or contaminate the water supply.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:22.000
But each of these attacks essentially enshrines&nbsp;
critical infrastructure as a legitimate target for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.760
a cyber attack. And at the end of the day, it's&nbsp;
civilian populations who would suffer the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.600
of those attacks. So it's, yeah, more dangerous.
So this is one of my questions about how this fits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.640
into warfare more broadly, because, you know, one&nbsp;
of the things that's happened over the last 10 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:44.880
15 years is we've heard more about and it actually&nbsp;
has moved in the direction of the idea of a.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.360
That's like a boots on the ground war, the likes&nbsp;
of which, you know, certainly the first Gulf War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.600
to a degree, the 2003 Iraq War, things are moving&nbsp;
away from that. Drone warfare is certainly part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.680
it, but also so is cyber warfare. But a foreign&nbsp;
power, for example, shutting off electricity in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.560
a large part of the United States. We understand&nbsp;
the chaos that it will create. We understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:13.120
damage it can do. But how would it fit into a&nbsp;
broader war in the sense of what would it be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:19.440
part of trying to accomplish for the foreign power&nbsp;
that does it? I'm guessing it's not shut the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.320
off so they can steal Florida from us and make&nbsp;
it part of some other country. There must be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:29.760
what would be the goal or how would it fit in?
Yeah, it's a really good question and it's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:37.120
important question because you don't want to&nbsp;
overhype the threat. Right. But it is the reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.640
that Russia has been targeting our grid&nbsp;
for several years now. A few years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:49.600
the Department of Homeland Security published a&nbsp;
screenshot that literally showed Russian hackers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:55.040
inside our power plants with their fingers on&nbsp;
the switches that could turn off the lights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.280
So we know that they have gotten into&nbsp;
these systems, which begs the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.080
what are they doing there? At what point will&nbsp;
they see some kind of incentive or motivation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:13.440
to turn off the lights? I do think that this is&nbsp;
all battlefield preparation for some future event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:21.680
whether it's a declaration of war. And this would&nbsp;
be one element of that warfare turning off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:28.160
lights or shutting off the communication systems&nbsp;
in the event that geopolitical tensions got so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:33.680
heated that one of these countries felt&nbsp;
the need to do so. We have been hacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.000
into Russia. It's great. I broke that story with&nbsp;
my colleague David Sanger a couple of years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.400
partly as retaliation somewhat for the&nbsp;
election, but also for Russia's acts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:50.640
and incursions of our own grid. But we've been&nbsp;
doing it more as mutually assured destruction.&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.760
I was going to say, yeah, I was going to say&nbsp;
it reminds me of like during nuclear weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:01.920
development, a lot of people say, what is the&nbsp;
circumstance where we would ever use even ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.080
of these nukes? Like, what is that situation? And&nbsp;
it becomes this conversation of, well, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.040
if they did it, we would we would need to be in a&nbsp;
position to do it. And being in a position to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.360
it would disincentives them from doing it.
It it has a sort of similar feel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:22.480
as you mentioned, that, yes, it is our attempt at&nbsp;
mutually assured destruction in the digital age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:31.680
The problem is that nuclear analogies really don't&nbsp;
fit so neatly in cyber. For one, this is all code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.600
right, where we're attacking these systems&nbsp;
with code. There's a low barrier to entry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:43.920
You know, if we have a system, it is entirely&nbsp;
possible for that attack to be discovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:49.680
for those methods to be discovered, for that&nbsp;
code to be picked up and reverse engineered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:56.720
and retrofitted and used back on us. And in cyber,&nbsp;
what we've noticed over the last 10 years is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:03.920
people learn a lot from their adversary. You&nbsp;
know, we pulled off an attack with Israel on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:10.400
Iran's nuclear plant where we broke into their&nbsp;
nuclear facility and Anton's nuclear facility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.200
We dispatched code that jumped from&nbsp;
their Microsoft Windows systems into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:21.360
their industrial controllers. And we eventually&nbsp;
use that code to spin their uranium centrifuges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.760
out of control or slow them down. And&nbsp;
we ultimately destroyed a thousand of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:32.960
their centrifuges. No one has pulled off a cyber&nbsp;
attack, that level of sophistication since. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.240
you know, what our adversaries have learned&nbsp;
is they don't need to have the sophistication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:45.120
of that attack to do damage. A couple of years&nbsp;
after that attack, we saw Iran hit Saudi Aramco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:51.040
world's largest oil company, Saudi Arabia, with an&nbsp;
attack that just used rudimentary code to wipe out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:56.880
the data on their systems and replace it with an&nbsp;
image of a burning American flag. And so we're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:03.200
sort of learning after each other and each attack&nbsp;
has sort of build off the capabilities of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:10.000
last. And unlike nuclear, where you need fissile&nbsp;
material to drop a bomb, right. In cyber, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.040
just need a laptop and someone with the skills.&nbsp;
And increasingly you can purchase those skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:23.920
and code and capabilities off a gray market. So&nbsp;
it's really unregulated space. It is getting more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:28.720
and more dangerous, particularly as we digitize&nbsp;
more of our infrastructure. And the thing I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:36.480
people don't realize is that America might still&nbsp;
be the greatest offensive player in this realm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.520
Like I said, no one has pulled off anything close&nbsp;
to the attack. We pulled off on Iran more than 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:48.400
years ago. But we are also the. Most targeted, we&nbsp;
have more nation states and cyber criminals lined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:53.680
up at our borders, at our systems, knocking on the&nbsp;
door, getting in and a lot of cases and planting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.680 --> 00:11:00.960
back doors and where the most vulnerable in a lot&nbsp;
of cases because we are so wired much more so than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:06.720
adversaries like North Korea and Iran, et cetera.
So on that note, I mean, in the limited time we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:11.440
have left, the reaction of some might be, well,&nbsp;
we've got to pull all of our we have to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:16.800
backwards technologically. We have to get a lot of&nbsp;
stuff that's currently networked off of networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:24.080
a silo, things, et cetera. In in most instances,&nbsp;
that's that's not really a solution to problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:31.200
Would it be the wrong the wrong reaction to this?
No. I mean, I think we do need to step back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.560
think harder about the defense of these systems.&nbsp;
80 percent of America's critical infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:42.080
is owned and maintained by private companies.&nbsp;
And any attempt to sort of legislate that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:49.200
have better security has has always failed.&nbsp;
Lobbyists have always watered it down. So any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:53.760
attempt to secure our critical infrastructure&nbsp;
has come in the form of an executive order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:59.200
first under Obama and then there was one under&nbsp;
Trump. But really, you know, these are all just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:05.280
setting voluntary standards for these critical&nbsp;
infrastructure owners to up their security. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:11.040
unfortunately, a lot of these systems are&nbsp;
owned by small, medium sized businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:18.160
local municipal systems that simply don't have&nbsp;
the security budgets or resources of a PG&amp;E say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:24.320
here in California. So they have to rely on this&nbsp;
outside network of contractors and supervisors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:29.600
And the fact that they have to let those people&nbsp;
in remotely creates an opportunity for hackers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:35.360
So what do we do if we're not going to legislate&nbsp;
that these companies have to up their security?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:40.480
What do you do? And I think one of the things&nbsp;
that we need to start talking about is creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:46.160
better market incentives for these companies to up&nbsp;
their security, you know, giving tax credits maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.640
to companies who used to factor authentication&nbsp;
and have all of their software up to date and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.640 --> 00:13:01.040
passed with flying colors on a penetration testing&nbsp;
exam. Or perhaps it's time to exact penalties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:07.040
on companies who are not updating and patching&nbsp;
their systems and allowing anyone to remote. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.640
so I do actually think it's a really&nbsp;
important question to ask ourselves, OK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:16.560
if we're not going to let the government into&nbsp;
these private systems, if we're going to rely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:22.560
on the market, well, the market has always led&nbsp;
us down a path of further vulnerability because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:28.400
security has no return. Return on investment.
Yeah, it's the Deepwater Horizon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:32.800
the five hundred thousand dollar value that&nbsp;
if you install it everywhere you protect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:36.960
but it's unlikely you'll need it everywhere&nbsp;
and then they install it nowhere at all.&nbsp;

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:41.840
That's such a great analogy. I hadn't thought&nbsp;
of that, but that is the perfect analogy. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:47.840
so we really need to have these conversations&nbsp;
around how to lock up these systems. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:52.240
you know, there's been some conversations about&nbsp;
should we have a digital Geneva Convention?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:58.480
Should we set some kind of international norms&nbsp;
of behavior where we would agree not to hack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:03.440
Russia's power grid and they would agree&nbsp;
not to have cars or hospitals or elections?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:10.960
The problem is we, the United States, will&nbsp;
never agree to those norms because, one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:16.320
we are still we we still have a huge offensive&nbsp;
advantage. But also, too, we don't trust that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:23.280
our adversaries would hold to those agreements&nbsp;
because they outsource many of their attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:30.080
to cyber criminals, to contractors and&nbsp;
front companies. The attacks that we conduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:35.040
out of the United States come out of the Pentagon,&nbsp;
Cyber Command or maybe the NSA, if it's espionage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:41.600
or the other intelligence agencies. We don't rely&nbsp;
on some, you know, security engineer at Google and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:46.240
tap them on the shoulder at night and say tonight&nbsp;
you're working with us. We just don't have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:52.480
ability or plausible deniability. So we worry that&nbsp;
if we ever agree to these norms, that these other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:57.680
countries would just sort of say, well, we won't&nbsp;
do it, but they would outsource those attacks.&nbsp;

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:02.000
Right. We don't we don't really want to commit not&nbsp;
to doing it. And we wouldn't believe that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:06.480
commitment would be it would be trustworthy. They&nbsp;
may believe the exact same about this as well. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:12.720
it makes it a very complicated situation. We've&nbsp;
been speaking with Nicole Pearl Roth, who is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:17.040
cybersecurity journalist for The New York Times.
The book is This Is How They Tell Me The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:21.200
World Ends the Cyber Weapons Arms Race.&nbsp;
Nicole, really appreciate your time today.&nbsp;

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:25.272
Thank you so much for having me. The&nbsp;
